      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To begin troubleshooting a \"VPN Connection Timeout\" effectively, you must first ensure that the user's sticky settings are cleared by disconnecting the current session and pressing the Windows key followed by R to input mcpac, then closing this window to reset the networking stack entirely. After clearing these caches, guide the technician to open a Command Prompt with administrator privileges and type ping 1.1.1.1 followed by enter, and if those packets are returned, it confirms the local internet is healthy, allowing you to focus on the external tunnel rather than general connectivity. If the previous step fails, try restarting your machine and navigating to Device Manager under the network adapters section, then disabling and re-enabling the physical ethernet or wireless interface to force a fresh hardware negotiation with the router. Once the hardware is stable, update the VPN client to the absolute latest version available on your internal OmniCorp repository, as outdated drivers often fail to handshake correctly with the new encryption protocols deployed by the backend engineering team. Finally, instruct the user to contact your help desk via ticketing if the issue remains after these four distinct phases, as this triggers our automated diagnostic bot that can pull logs from your corporate firewall to determine if a specific server is down or rejecting your IP",
